{"text": "Lesson nine, three open frontiers and the conclusion. The stack is not finished. The survey names three problems that cut across the pillars. If you can state each one as a trade-off and say why it is still open, you have the last chapter of the exam. 1st, recall versus efficiency for subquadratic state. A fixed recurrent state is an information bottleneck. Unlike the key value cache, it cannot keep a lossless history. Needle in a haystack retrieval, long multi-turn logs, and hard in-context learning degrade. Hybrids such as Jamba rent back a little attention, and with it, they reintroduce a cache that still grows with context. They do not close the trade-off. The open problem is how to bound memory without losing non-local associative recall. 2nd, the lossless versus lossy line in parallel decoding. Speculation stays lossless only while the draft is aligned with the target. If the draft distribution drifts, the mean accepted Length falls toward zero, and verification is pure waste. Newer drafters move toward the verifier offline or in batches. A principled realignment on each request is still open. Diffusion and consistency models sit on the lossy side on purpose. They trade exact left-to-right conditionals for wide parallel blocks. Can the draft width or the number of denoising steps track live bandwidth? And can a small deviation from the autoregressive distribution be acceptable because the memory bus is the scarce resource? 3rd, error under composed compression. A plausible pipeline prune structure linearizes the remaining attention by distillation, then quantizes. Each stage was calibrated on the model that entered it. Total error can be much larger than the sum of the three pieces. A composition theory with predictable bounds is still open, especially off the data center. The conclusion restates the co-design claim. Key value layout has to work with virtual memory. Speculative loops have to meet low Precision kernels. Compression has to know which phase of a split network it is serving. These three frontiers are what still blocks cheap, real-time inference at global scale. That is the survey: four pillars, four bottlenecks, one cache, three open problems.", "language": "en", "duration": 136.368, "words": [{"text": "Lesson", "start": 0.14, "end": 0.42}, {"text": "nine,", "start": 0.52, "end": 0.88}, {"text": "three", "start": 1.46, "end": 1.721}, {"text": "open", "start": 1.861, "end": 2.081}, {"text": "frontiers", "start": 2.161, "end": 2.821}, {"text": "and", "start": 3.041, "end": 3.121}, {"text": "the", "start": 3.141, "end": 3.221}, {"text": "conclusion.", "start": 3.241, "end": 3.841}, {"text": "The", "start": 4.461, "end": 4.542}, {"text": "stack", "start": 4.622, "end": 4.922}, {"text": "is", "start": 4.982, "end": 5.062}, {"text": "not", "start": 5.142, "end": 5.322}, {"text": "finished.", "start": 5.402, "end": 5.822}, {"text": "The", "start": 6.262, "end": 6.342}, {"text": "survey", "start": 6.442, "end": 6.742}, {"text": "names", "start": 6.802, "end": 7.062}, {"text": "three", "start": 7.142, "end": 7.362}, {"text": "problems", "start": 7.422, "end": 7.923}, {"text": "that", "start": 7.963, "end": 8.063}, {"text": "cut", "start": 8.123, "end": 8.283}, {"text": "across", "start": 8.303, "end": 8.643}, {"text": "the", "start": 8.683, "end": 8.783}, {"text": "pillars.", "start": 8.823, "end": 9.223}, {"text": "If", "start": 9.803, "end": 9.883}, {"text": "you", "start": 9.903, "end": 10.003}, {"text": "can", "start": 10.023, "end": 10.123}, {"text": "state", "start": 10.203, "end": 10.483}, {"text": "each", "start": 10.564, "end": 10.724}, {"text": "one", "start": 10.844, "end": 10.964}, {"text": "as", "start": 10.984, "end": 11.064}, {"text": "a", "start": 11.104, "end": 11.124}, {"text": "trade-off", "start": 11.204, "end": 11.724}, {"text": "and", "start": 12.084, "end": 12.184}, {"text": "say", "start": 12.244, "end": 12.384}, {"text": "why", "start": 12.444, "end": 12.624}, {"text": "it", "start"